<aiml version="1.0">
<category>
<pattern>WHEN WAS MORRIS WORM RELEASED</pattern>
<template>The Morris worm was released on November 2, 1988, by Robert Tappan Morris at Cornell University.</template>
</category>
<category>
<pattern>WHAT WAS TARGET SYSTEM OF MORRIS WORM</pattern>
<template>The worm primarily affected VAX computers and Sun-3 workstations running Berkeley UNIX 4.2 and 4.3.</template>
</category>
<category>
<pattern>WHAT WERE EXPLOITATION VECTORS OF MORRIS WORM</pattern>
<template>The worm exploited a debug mode hole in sendmail, a buffer overflow in the finger service, and the rexec/rsh feature enabling transitive trust.</template>
</category>
<category>
<pattern>WHAT WAS REPLICATION FLAW OF MORRIS WORM</pattern>
<template>The worm was programmed to copy itself 14% of the time regardless of infection status, causing repeated infections and system slowdowns.</template>
</category>
<category>
<pattern>WHAT WAS IMPACT OF MORRIS WORM</pattern>
<template>Estimates range from 6,000 to 10,000 infected computers, with economic impact between $100,000 and $10,000,000.</template>
</category>
<category>
<pattern>WHAT WAS LEGAL OUTCOME OF MORRIS WORM</pattern>
<template>Morris was the first person convicted under the 1986 Computer Fraud and Abuse Act, sentenced to three years' probation, 400 hours of community service, and fines totaling $13,326.</template>
</category>
<category>
<pattern>WHAT WAS AFTERMATH OF MORRIS WORM</pattern>
<template>The incident prompted the creation of the CERT/CC at Carnegie Mellon University and the Phage mailing list for coordinating network emergency responses.</template>
</category>
</aiml>
